Scientific problem:
Discover an interpretable, physics-informed descriptor set that identifies electrides.
Electrides are ionic compounds in which some valence electrons are not bound to any atom but occupy
interstitial cavities, channels or layers and act as anions (anionic electrons). Using ONLY the precomputed
structural and chemical features of the labeled structures (catalog in the system prompt), find the set of features
whose combination separates electrides from non-electrides. Justify every selected feature physically, supported by
literature where possible. A model is fitted on your features and evaluated deterministically after every round and the
evaluation is returned to you: use it, with the literature, to choose each new feature set. State what the
available features cannot capture (e.g. where the excess electrons actually localize) and which additional
descriptors would be needed.

Round 4/5. Peers' models and findings from the PREVIOUS round (critique, don't blindly copy):
{
  "A4": {
    "answer": "The recent descriptor\u2011development study (arXiv:2605.11724) explicitly proposes using geometric metrics of interstitial space \u2013 such as the number of void\u2011center candidates and clearance statistics \u2013 as high\u2011throughput descriptors for electride discovery.  This supports keeping the scalar feature *candidate_count_before_output_limit* as a proxy for how many accessible interstitial sites a crystal provides.  The same work also stresses the importance of (i) a highly electropositive composition, (ii) a low\u2011electronegativity environment that leaves excess electrons unbound, (iii) a spacious lattice that can host those electrons, and (iv) a limited set of charge\u2011balanced oxidation\u2011state solutions indicating excess electrons.  The evaluation of our previous eight\u2011feature set showed that *pauling_electronegativity__min*, *pauling_electronegativity__std*, *max_clearance_A* and *f_block_site_fraction* contributed little, so they are removed.  The remaining features directly map onto the four physics pillars and are mutually complementary, giving the model a clearer decision surface and a chance to improve ROC\u2011AUC beyond the 0.93 achieved previously.",
