  },
  "A2": {
    "answer": "The abstracts retrieved confirm that a contrast between very electropositive and more electronegative elements is a key driver of interstitial electron localisation. Li6P is described as a combination of a weakly electronegative P and strongly electropositive Li, while the sodalite\u2011derived electride study explicitly states that \u201ca lower electronegativity of surrounding cations drives greater electron localisation\u201d. The layered SnO/PbO electrides further note that higher cation electronegativity reduces localisation, implying that the spread of electronegativities within a compound modulates electride character. Therefore, beyond the minimum electronegativity, the standard deviation of the Pauling electronegativities (pauling_electronegativity__std) should capture this contrast and improve discrimination.\n\nBuilding on the best\u2011performing Round\u20112 set, I add pauling_electronegativity__std while retaining the other six descriptors that already encode electropositivity, cavity size, cavity heterogeneity, and the metallic framework. This yields a seven\u2011feature, physics\u2011guided descriptor set that directly reflects (i) the presence of electropositive s\u2011block cations, (ii) a low minimum EN, (iii) a large EN spread, (iv) large and on\u2011average accessible interstitial voids, (v) heterogeneity of those voids, (vi) a metallic backbone, and (vii) the absolute size of the largest void.",
